.TH LIL 1
.SH NAME
lil \- little Limbo: an interpreted subset, in place of a shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lil
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lil
reads statements in a small subset of Limbo and runs them as they are
typed.
It exists for boards where a shell does not fit \- chiefly
.IR stm32f407 ,
which has 147456 bytes for the Dis pool and whose
.I mkfile
records that
.B /dis/sh.dis
asks for 73536 of them for its instruction array alone.
.PP
The size of the shell is not the reason, though.  Execution-in-place
puts an instruction array in flash, which is the only reason a shell
runs on that board at all, and it would do the same for a smaller one.
What XIP does not help with is the shell's semantics: a command is a
process and a redirection is an open, so
.PP
.EX
	echo rssioffset -90 >/dev/radio0/ctl
.EE
.PP
forks twice to write eleven bytes.  The board's own configuration file
records where that ends \-
.B "no procs for kproc"
\- and
.B tools/radio/board.py
records the same failure from the other side: a monitor running one
probe every two seconds killed the shell in a few minutes.
.PP
What is wanted instead is a language, in which a file is opened once,
kept in a variable, and written to as often as one likes:
.PP
.EX
	ctl := open("/dev/radio0/ctl", "w")
	write(ctl, "freq 915000000")
	write(ctl, "rate 9600")
	print(slurp("/dev/radio0/stats"))
.EE
.PP
The syntax is Limbo's because that is the language this tree is written
in, so that what is typed at a console and what is compiled into a
.B .dis
look alike.  A Forth would have been smaller and a Tcl more uniform;
both would have been one more language to know for no gain.
.SS Statements
.TP
.BI x " := e"
Introduce
.I x
and give it the value of
.IR e .
.TP
.BI x " = e"
Assign to an
.I x
that already exists.  A name that does not is an error and not a new
variable: a mistyped name on the left of
.B =
otherwise makes a variable nobody reads, and the write goes nowhere,
silently, which is the one class of mistake a console session cannot
see.
.TP
.BI if( e ) { ... } " else " { ... }
The
.B else
must begin on the same line as the closing brace of the
.B if
body.  A line is read as complete once its brackets balance, and there
is nothing to look ahead with.
.TP
.BI while( e ) { ... }
Bounded by time, not by a count of iterations: see
.B timelimit
below.
.PP
Statements are separated by
.B ;
or by a newline, and a line whose brackets do not balance is continued,
so a loop can be typed over several lines.
.B #
begins a comment.
.SS Values
There are three kinds: an integer, a string, and an open file.
That is deliberate and not a stage on the way to more.  Holding a file
descriptor across statements is the whole purpose; a program that wants
lists, tuples, ADTs or functions of its own should be compiled.
Integers may be written in decimal, or in hex as
.B 0x0a
or
.BR 16r0a ,
because every register and sync word on this bench is written that way.
.B +
on a string concatenates, and comparisons work on both kinds.
.SS Builtins
.TP
.BI open( path [, mode ])
.I Mode
is
.BR r ,
.B w
or
.BR rw ,
and defaults to
.BR r .
.TP
.BI create( path )
.TP
.BI read( fd [, n ])
One read of at most
.I n
bytes, 256 by default.  This is what a device file wants: a Styx read
boundary is a message boundary, so a short read is a whole answer.
.TP
.BI readall( fd )
Read until end of file.  A device file need never give one.
.TP
.BI slurp( path )
Open, read to end of file, and close.  This is
.IR cat (1),
and it is most of what the shell was used for here.
.TP
.BI write( fd , " e " ...)
Write the concatenation of its arguments, with no newline added.  One
call is one write, and so one Styx message.
.TP
.BI close( x )
.I X
must be the name of a variable.  Limbo shuts a file when the last
reference to it goes, and the reference that matters is the one in the
variable table, so this is a statement rather than a builtin: written as
a builtin it was handed the value and not the name, and reported success
while closing nothing.
.TP
.BI dir( path )
The names in a directory, one to a line, with
.B /
after those that are themselves directories.
.TP
.BI bind( from , " to" [, " flag" ])
.I Flag
is
.BR a ,
.B b
or
.BR c ,
for after, before and create; replace is the default.
.TP
.BI sleep( ms )
.TP
.B millisec()
.TP
.BI len( e )
.TP
.BI timelimit([ ms ])
How long one
.B while
may run, 60000 by default; returns the bound.
.B timelimit(0)
removes it.
.PP
The bound is in time and not in iterations because a count is not a
timeout: what matters is how long the console goes undrained.  This
board's
.IR devcons (3)
has no interrupt character \- nothing in
.B kbdputc()
turns a keystroke into a note \- so a loop that does not end can only be
left by resetting the board, and a mistyped condition should not cost a
power cycle.  When the bound trips it says so, rather than quietly
returning a half-finished loop.
.SS What it does not have
No functions of one's own, no lists, tuples or declared types, no
.BR for ,
no
.B &&
or
.BR || ,
no
.B ~
or regular expressions, no globbing, no pipes and no redirection.  The
last three are absent on purpose: they are the shell semantics this
exists to avoid.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/lil.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR limbo (1)
.SH BUGS
There is no syntax tree.  A
.B while
remembers the source text of its condition and body and re-scans them
each time round, which is slow; it is the trade a Forth makes too, on a
machine where a human types the program one line at a time.
.PP
.B print
is the only way to see a value; there is no automatic printing of a bare
expression.
.SH VERIFIED
What has actually been run, and what has not.
.PP
Every statement form and every builtin above has been exercised under
the host
.IR emu (1)
on a 64-bit Dis tree: declaration and assignment, hex and negative
literals, string and integer comparison,
.B if
with and without
.BR else ,
.B while
including its time bound tripping and being raised,
.BR dir ,
a
.B create
written to twice through one held-open descriptor and read back, and
.B close
followed by a
.B read
that correctly fails.  The module compiles clean with warnings enabled
at both Dis word sizes, and is 12283 bytes at
.BR DISWORD=4 .
.PP
Not yet done: it has not been run on the
.I stm32f407
kernel, under
.IR qemu (1)
or on silicon, so the claim that it survives where the shell did not is
so far an argument from how it is built and not a measurement.  The
comparison that would settle it is
.BR board.py 's
own recorded failure \- one probe every two seconds for several minutes
\- run again in
.IR lil .
.PP
One thing found while testing it is not
.IR lil 's
and matters more.  Under this tree's
.IR emu ,
every Dis heap allocation appears never to be reclaimed: a compiled
Limbo program whose loop body is nothing but
.B t := s[1:]
grows the live allocation count in
.B /dev/memory
by exactly one per iteration and dies at
.B "out of memory: heap"
after about 260000 of them, having extended the arena rather than
collected.  The same holds for string concatenation, for
.BR "array of byte" ,
and for a
.BR ref ,
whether the variable is declared inside the loop, outside it, or
returned from a function.  If that also holds on the board then it, and
not the cost of forking, is why the shell there dies, and no interpreter
can help.  That is untested on the board and is the first thing to
measure there.
